# M2. Torque on writes goal = present position first. The simulated servos use the dangerous default: turning
# torque on drives to whatever goal is in the register, so a goal left there must be rewritten first.
# The terminal only prepares it: the walker shows and says it, and only a handle Confirm press 2 s or more
# after that starts it. A press in the first 2 s is ignored (and starts that button's 2 s again).
expect motion mode ready
key stray-goal-left
expect sim left follower goals moved away
key torque-on-left
expect motion target arm.torque operator_cli: Turn on the left arm's motors
expect spoke Turn on the left arm's motors. Park the walker and lock the brakes, then press Confirm.
absent 1000 sim torque-on
key c
expect motion press-ignored ignore_too_early
absent 1200 sim torque-on
key c
expect motion press-ignored ignore_too_early
wait 2100
key c
expect sim torque-on left follower servo 1 goal-at-present
expect sim torque-on left follower servo 3 goal-at-present
expect sim torque-on left follower servo 6 goal-at-present
expect motion torque left on
absent 500 sim goal-away
# Single use: another Confirm press doesn't run it again.
wait 2100
key c
absent 1000 motion torque left on
key q
